  "question": {
    "text": "If in the expansion of $(a-2 b)^{\\mathrm{n}}$, the sum of the 5 th and 6 th term is zero, then the value of $\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{b}}$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "bWiDP",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{n}-4}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "S8Bdk",
      "text": "$\\frac{2(n-4)}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "06OfA",
      "text": "$\\frac{5}{n-4}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "FgdMC",
      "text": "$\\frac{5}{2(n-4)}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$$\n\\text { Hints: } \\begin{gathered}\n(a-2 b)^{\\mathrm{n}}=\\sum_{\\mathrm{r}=0}^{\\mathrm{n}}{ }^{\\mathrm{n}} \\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{r}}(a)^{\\mathrm{n}-\\mathrm{r}}(-2 \\mathrm{~b})^{\\mathrm{r}} \\\\\n\\mathrm{t}_5+\\mathrm{t}_6=0\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\Rightarrow{ }^{\\mathrm{n}} \\mathrm{C}_4(\\mathrm{a})^{\\mathrm{n}-4}(-2 \\mathrm{~b})^4+{ }^{\\mathrm{n}} \\mathrm{C}_5(\\mathrm{a})^{\\mathrm{n}-5}(-2 \\mathrm{~b})^5=0 \\Rightarrow \\frac{\\mathrm{n} !}{4 !(\\mathrm{n}-4) !} \\mathrm{a}^{\\mathrm{n}-4}(-2 \\mathrm{~b})^4=-\\frac{\\mathrm{n} !}{5 !(\\mathrm{n}-5) !}(\\mathrm{a})^{\\mathrm{n}-5}(-2 \\mathrm{~b})^5 \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{(\\mathrm{n}-4)} \\times \\mathrm{a}=\\frac{-1}{5}(-2 \\mathrm{~b}) \\Rightarrow \\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{b}}=\\frac{2(\\mathrm{n}-4)}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
